# resources/ - not in the repository any more. The Nextor kernel and system
# files moved to src/nextor/, where the rest of what we ship lives; the other
# four folders held nothing but a README telling you to fill them.
#
# Optional assets still have a place, it just is not made for you. The printer
# looks in resources/fonts/ for a TTF and resources/font-roms/ for a kanji ROM,
# and skips that output if neither is there. Create the folder if you want it.
resources/

# MSX virtual disk images, anywhere. This used to be anchored to the root, back
# when dist/disk/ carried a committed image and a bare *.img would have needed
# an exception to undo. No image is shipped now - serve.sh builds one on first
# run - so there is nothing to carve out, and a 128MB disk full of somebody's
# games is the last thing that should reach a public repository by accident.
*.img

# disk server <-> disk_put.sh coordination sidecars (transient)
*.srv
*.hold
*.held
*.dmg

# output/ - what the MSX printed, coming back. Matched by name rather than by
# path: serve.sh puts it at dist/output/, the tools default to ../output beside
# themselves, and --output moves it anywhere. It is also the one thing under
# dist/ that is not committed - cartridge/ is what you flash, disk/ is what you
# serve, output/ is what comes back, and no version of your printouts belongs in
# a repository.
output/
capture/

# Pico SDK / CMake build output (intermediates, for the tools)
build/
build-*/
cmake-build-*/
CMakeFiles/
CMakeCache.txt
compile_commands.json
*.elf
*.elf.map
*.bin
*.hex
*.dis
# *.uf2 is deliberately absent. A flashable image belongs in the repository -
# dist/cartridge/ ships one - and every .uf2 that should be ignored lives under
# a build/ directory, which the rule above already covers. Sweeping the
# extension globally means re-including things afterwards, and that exception
# list is what silently drops a folder added later.

# MSX build output
src/picodock-menu/build/
src/picodock-menu/dist/
src/msx-tools/version.inc

# Node.js packages, installed on first run, never committed
node_modules/

# Editors / OS
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/

# dist/disk/user-files/ is the user's own: whatever is dropped in there goes onto
# the next image make-disk.sh builds. Nothing in it is ours, not even a README -
# the top-level README.md explains the folder, and sync-disk.sh makes it when it
# is missing. So the folder is not in a fresh clone at all, which is the honest
# state: it has nothing in it until you put something there.
/dist/disk/user-files/*

# ...and the two things that grow out of what ships. picodock.org.uf2 is this
# project's bare cartridge and is committed; what sits beside it without .org is
# make-uf2.sh over your roms/, so rebuild it as often as you like and the
# original stays. There is no disk to match: no image is committed, because
# 128 MB of which 30 KB is content does not belong in a repository. serve.sh
# builds picodock.img on first run, and deleting it is how you start over.
/dist/disk/picodock.img
/dist/cartridge/picodock.uf2

# ...except the ROMs themselves.
#
# dist/ is committed whole - that is deliberate, so a clone is a working
# PicoDock. These two lines are the one hole in it, and they are not a tidiness
# preference: a .rom is rarely yours to redistribute, and this is a public
# repository.
#
# Delete this line if you want them in. Nothing else depends on it.
dist/cartridge/roms/*

# ...and ROM dumps anywhere else, because roms/ is only where they are *supposed*
# to go. One dropped in the repository root or beside the source would otherwise
# be tracked, and the rule above would have looked like it was doing its job.
# Both spellings: this checkout is case-insensitive and Linux is not.
*.rom
*.ROM
# The three that are ours to ship, named back in.
!src/nextor/Nextor-2.1.4.SunriseIDE.MasterOnly.ROM
!src/nextor/Nextor-2.1.4.SunriseIDE.MasterOnly.PicoDock.ROM
!src/picodock-menu/reference/menu.rom
# ...and the images built over them. A UF2 carries the ROM files *and their
# names* inside it, so committing one republishes whatever was in roms/ at the
# time - which is the same problem as committing the ROMs, one step removed.
#
# So the rule is a whitelist, not a blacklist: every .uf2 under cartridge/ is
# ignored except picodock.uf2, which is the one build that carries NO ROMs
# (src/build_diskprint.sh - our Nextor entry and nothing else). A new variant
# therefore stays out of git by default, which is the safe direction to fail.
*.uf2
!dist/cartridge/picodock.org.uf2

# The READMEs under dist/. Upstream keeps them - they explain the folder to
# someone reading that source tree - but here the top-level README.md is the one
# document an end user reads, and five more scattered through dist/ only compete
# with it. Ignored rather than merely deleted because dist/disk/tools/README.md
# is generated by src/stage_dist.sh on every build: without this, running the
# build would quietly offer it back.
/dist/README.md
/dist/cartridge/README.md
/dist/cartridge/roms/README.md
/dist/disk/tools/README.md
/dist/node/README.md
/dist/disk/user-files/README.md
